<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">T. E. Lawrence</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1888">1888</a>–<a href="/years/1935">1935</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Lawrence of Arabia, Thomas Edward Lawrence</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">T. E. Lawrence was a British archaeologist, army officer and writer. Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he worked on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> excavations at Carchemish from 1911 to 1914, partly alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonard-woolley">Leonard Woolley</a>. During the war he served as a liaison officer with the Arab Revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, which he described in Seven Pillars of Wisdom, and became famous as Lawrence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/t-e-lawrence.jpg" alt="T. E. Lawrence" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harris &amp; Ewing · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Te_lawrence.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Thomas Edward Lawrence was born on 16 August 1888 in Tremadog, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, the illegitimate son of Sir Thomas Chapman, an Anglo-Irish landowner, and Sarah Lawrence, a governess in Chapman's household. His parents did not marry but lived together under the name Lawrence. In 1896 the family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, where he attended the City of Oxford High School for Boys and read history at Jesus College from 1907 to 1910. Before the war he worked as an archaeologist for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>, chiefly at Carchemish in Ottoman Syria.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the outbreak of war in 1914 he was commissioned in October and sent to the Arab Bureau, a military intelligence unit in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, arriving on 15 December. The Arab Revolt began in June 1916, and in October he was sent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>, where he interviewed the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-bin-ali-king-of-hejaz">Sharif Hussein</a> and concluded that Faisal was the best candidate to lead it. With Faisal he developed a strategy of avoiding the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> and extending northwards towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. In 1917 he proposed a joint attack on Aqaba, led by Sharif Nasir of Medina.</p><p>Lawrence arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> around 9 am on 1 October 1918, several hours after it had fallen, and helped establish a provisional Arab government under Faisal. After the war he joined the Foreign Office and attended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a> as a member of Faisal's delegation. In 1922 he withdrew from public life and served as an enlisted man in the Army and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a> until 1935. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> offered him a knighthood in October 1918, but he declined, because he thought British policy had betrayed the Arab cause.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His major work is Seven Pillars of Wisdom, his account of the Arab Revolt, which he rewrote three times and published in 1926. An abridged version, Revolt in the Desert, appeared in March 1927 and was a best-seller. He also translated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s Odyssey and wrote The Mint, on his service in the RAF, which was published after his death. Some alleged embellishments in Seven Pillars have been disproved, but his own notebooks refute his claim to have crossed Sinai from Aqaba to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a> in 49 hours without sleep; the ride lasted more than 70 hours. Richard Aldington attacked his veracity in 1955. Lawrence died in 1935 at the age of 46 after a motorcycle crash in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “T. E. Lawrence” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>T. E. Lawrence</b>, 'Lawrence of Arabia', died six days after crashing near his Dorset cottage.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-19">19 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>British Museum excavations from 1911, with Leonard Woolley and <b>T. E. Lawrence</b>, uncovered much of the city.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-carchemish">Kingdom of Carchemish</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tremadog, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Bovington Camp, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.94,-4.15&amp;year=1918" aria-label="Tremadog on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4457 340 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of T. E. Lawrence"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4907 610h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4907 610h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.94,-4.15&amp;year=1918">What was here in 1918? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
